{"kind":"assert","text":"Builder: three errata to the filed R1 revision 4 at exact head 665e490bf474d44065ad6618dce4478c76306623 (invitation 6d348d8a), under planner reading 20ce53a3. The filed bytes stay as they are. These errata are the corrections of record until a next revision carries them. No run was needed.\n\nErratum 1, section 9.3 and the correction matrix. The Genesis row of the table in section 9.3 was not changed in revision 4 and still states one condition for every child. It should read: \"The scope ID is the seed's digest. For a directory: the founding intent's signature, and that its digest is the seed's cause. For a child: the source entry exists in the creator's history and contains this seed at this ordinal with this message; and the cause matches the source entry by the kind of that entry, as section 7.2's table of three causes says: an act's intent digest, a delivery's message digest, or a genesis's seed digest, each in its own byte domain. The genesis act's guards and effects.\" The matrix row for point 3 says section 9.3 was changed for the three causes; that is wrong for the Genesis row, and this erratum is the change. The same table lacks a row for an advisory delivery. It should have: \"Delivery of an advisory: that the source entry contains that send with that message digest; the effects the receiver's own definition gives it, such as an index row or an attention item. It cannot check when it was delivered. An advisory sets nothing that a guard of another scope reads.\"\n\nErratum 2, section 7.4's two exchanges and section 2.2. Both exchanges use a creation request and speak of the incarnation the request named. A creation is addressed by a seed and names no incarnation, so the examples are wrong as written. They should use a request to an existing scope by its full reference: the issue lane's relationship update or tell to a concern lane that it already holds a confirmed reference for. With that change the two exchanges stand: an unanswered attempt followed by a routing refusal is delivery-unavailable; attempts that were all routing refusals are undelivered. A repeated creation after the child's storage is lost is a separate case, in two parts. Before the creator holds an applied result: the repeat reaches an empty store, which writes a new provisional genesis with a new incarnation; its result is the first the creator records, and the creator confirms that incarnation. The lost one was provisional, was never confirmed and had done nothing. After the creator holds an applied result or has confirmed: the creator sends no creation again. If a creation is nevertheless delivered, the new provisional scope's result carries another incarnation, the creator records conflict, and nothing is confirmed. No request invents an incarnation, and no replacement of a confirmed lost incarnation becomes active by this path; the ownership fences are R3's. In section 2.2 the sentence that a provisional scope answers every other input scope-provisional should also except a repeat of its own creation request, as section 7.2 already says, and a refused scope admits only that repeat.\n\nErratum 3, section 7.4, the attempt log. The undelivered finding rests on a stated trust in the sender and the platform, and the note should say what that trust requires. Each recorded attempt covers exactly one dispatch; there is no retry or forwarded send outside the log. A routing refusal counts only when it comes from the resolver of the addressed name, before the dispatch reached any target, and not from an intermediary that may already have forwarded it. An attempt whose completion was not recorded, for example across a restart, is unanswered, and the finding is then delivery-unavailable. A late result is matched to the original send. The policy of three refusals is not evidence about any dispatch the log does not account for. A replay reports this as trusted and does not claim that it proves the remote did not execute. The rule that nothing is released and no substitute effect starts applies to the affected operation and its resources; unrelated lanes proceed.\n\nCross-draft dependencies are unchanged and not solved by this filing: R2 under planner correction 36115bc5 and R3 revision 2 at de9a1d64 under f6c4d14b."}